.TH CBMAP_CONSTRUCT_CC_SCOPED 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cbmap_construct_cc_scoped \- declare and create an ordered map with a custom comparison procedure, destroyed on scope exit
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>

cbmap_construct_cc_scoped(hm_name, key_t, val_t, custom_comparison_proc);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares an ordered map handle named hm_name, together with the companion
type variables that carry key_t and val_t to every later type-inferred
macro, and then creates a map whose keys custom_comparison_proc orders
instead of the default order for key_t. The macro also attaches the
cleanup attribute, which destroys the map on every exit from the enclosing
scope, an early return included.

The macro is cbmap_construct_cc(3) plus that cleanup attribute; nothing else
differs. cbmap_construct_scoped(3) is the same macro with the default order,
cbmap_construct_mp_scoped(3) takes an allocator in place of a comparison
procedure, and cbmap_construct_full_scoped(3) takes both.

A struct key_t needs this scoped variant, because the default order for a
key type that the map does not recognize is a raw comparison of the
representation of the key.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name                 Name for the map handle to declare
key_t                   Key type. It tells every later macro how to read
                        a key, and tells the container which width to
                        expect and which alignment to give a stored key
val_t                   Value type. Only the type-inferred macros read
                        it; the library never gives it to the container
custom_comparison_proc  A ccol_comparison_proc_t, or NULL for the
                        default order for key_t
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. This macro declares variables and does not expand to an expression.
.fi
.SH "CUSTOM COMPARISON"
.nf
The procedure gets pointers to two keys. It returns a negative value when
the first key orders before the second key, a positive value when the
first key orders after the second key, and zero when the map must treat
the two keys as the same key. A zero makes a call an update instead of an
insert, so the procedure defines both key equality and key order.

The procedure must give a strict weak order over key_t, and that order must
not change while the map holds elements. Because the procedure gets two
bare pointers and no lengths, it can only read a width that key_t fixes,
so the map validates the width of an incoming key against key_t and
aligns its stored keys for key_t.

The procedure must stay callable for the whole life of the map, which here
ends at the closing brace of the enclosing scope.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro stops the program through ccol_fatal_err() if the creation fails
The macro needs GCC or Clang, because it uses the cleanup attribute
The macro uses the default allocator
The walk visits the elements in ascending key order, by the order of this
procedure. cbmap_begin_iter(3) describes that contract
The exit of the scope destroys the map and clears the handle, so you can
also call cbmap_destroy(3) earlier in the scope
The companion type variables follow the scope of the handle, so a function
that receives the handle needs cbmap_redeclare(3) there
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
The handle must not outlive its scope. Do not return it, and do not store
it in a place that outlives the scope: either action gives out a handle
whose map the cleanup attribute frees at the exit of the scope.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
typedef struct { int major; int minor; } version;
int version_cmp(const void *a, const void *b);

void summarize(version *v, size_t n) {
    cbmap_construct_cc_scoped(releases, version, int, version_cmp);
    int one = 1;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        cbmap_insert(releases, v[i], one);
    }
}                             /* The macro destroys the releases map here */
.fi
